### How do you remove duplicates while keeping insertion order?

The idiomatic one-liner is:

```java
List<String> deduped = new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(original));
```

This works because LinkedHashSet rejects duplicates (keeping the first occurrence) and maintains insertion order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DeduplicationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> transactionIds = List.of(
            "TXN-001", "TXN-002", "TXN-003", "TXN-001", "TXN-004", "TXN-002", "TXN-005"
        );
        System.out.println("Original: " + transactionIds);
        System.out.println("Size: " + transactionIds.size());

        // Method 1: LinkedHashSet one-liner
        List<String> deduped1 = new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(transactionIds));
        System.out.println("\nLinkedHashSet: " + deduped1);

        // Method 2: Stream distinct()
        List<String> deduped2 = transactionIds.stream().distinct().toList();
        System.out.println("Stream:        " + deduped2);

        // Method 3: HashSet – loses order!
        List<String> unordered = new ArrayList<>(new HashSet<>(transactionIds));
        System.out.println("HashSet:       " + unordered);

        // Method 4: TreeSet – sorts instead of preserving order
        List<String> sorted = new ArrayList<>(new TreeSet<>(transactionIds));
        System.out.println("TreeSet:       " + sorted);
    }
}
```

```text
Original: [TXN-001, TXN-002, TXN-003, TXN-001, TXN-004, TXN-002, TXN-005]
Size: 7

LinkedHashSet: [TXN-001, TXN-002, TXN-003, TXN-004, TXN-005]
Stream:        [TXN-001, TXN-002, TXN-003, TXN-004, TXN-005]
HashSet:       [TXN-003, TXN-004, TXN-005, TXN-001, TXN-002]
TreeSet:       [TXN-001, TXN-002, TXN-003, TXN-004, TXN-005]
```

### Which method should you use?

| Method | Preserves order | Time | Space | Returns |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| new LinkedHashSet<>(list) | Yes (insertion) | O(n) | O(n) | Set |
| list.stream().distinct().toList() | Yes (encounter) | O(n) | O(n) | List |
| new HashSet<>(list) | No | O(n) | O(n) | Set |
| new TreeSet<>(list) | Sorted | O(n log n) | O(n) | SortedSet |

For most cases, the LinkedHashSet one-liner or `stream().distinct()` are the best choices.

### How do you deduplicate by a specific field?

When deduplicating complex objects, you may want to keep the first occurrence based on a specific field rather than the entire object.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FieldDeduplicationDemo {
    record Transaction(String id, String account, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transaction> transactions = List.of(
            new Transaction("TXN-001", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Transaction("TXN-002", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("25000")),  // same account
            new Transaction("TXN-003", "CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Transaction("TXN-004", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("10000"))   // same account
        );

        // Keep first transaction per account
        LinkedHashMap<String, Transaction> byAccount = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (Transaction txn : transactions) {
            byAccount.putIfAbsent(txn.account(), txn);
        }
        List<Transaction> uniqueByAccount = new ArrayList<>(byAccount.values());
        System.out.println("Unique by account:");
        uniqueByAccount.forEach(t ->
            System.out.println("  " + t.id() + " " + t.account() + " " + t.amount()));

        // Stream equivalent using Collectors.toMap with merge function
        Map<String, Transaction> streamResult = transactions.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                Transaction::account,
                t -> t,
                (first, second) -> first, // keep first occurrence
                LinkedHashMap::new         // preserve order
            ));
    }
}
```

```text
Unique by account:
  TXN-001 SAV2001 50000
  TXN-003 CUR3001 75000
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Using HashSet when order matters

```java
// WRONG: loses order
List<String> deduped = new ArrayList<>(new HashSet<>(list));
// Output order is unpredictable!

// CORRECT: preserves order
List<String> deduped = new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(list));
```

#### Mistake 2: Forgetting that distinct() on parallel streams may not preserve order

```java
// distinct() on ordered sequential stream: preserves encounter order
list.stream().distinct().toList(); // order preserved

// distinct() on parallel stream: may not preserve order
list.parallelStream().distinct().toList(); // order NOT guaranteed
// Use forEachOrdered or .sequential() if order matters
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **distinct() uses a LinkedHashSet internally.** The stream distinct() operation maintains a LinkedHashSet of seen elements under the hood for ordered sequential streams.

- **Large-scale dedup.** For very large datasets that do not fit in memory, consider external sorting + sequential dedup, or Bloom filters for approximate dedup.

- **Collectors.toCollection(LinkedHashSet::new).** In streams, you can collect directly to a LinkedHashSet: `stream.collect(Collectors.toCollection(LinkedHashSet::new))`.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you remove duplicates from a List?" – new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(list)).
- "Does stream().distinct() preserve order?" – Yes, for ordered sequential streams.
- "How do you deduplicate by a field?" – Use LinkedHashMap with putIfAbsent or Collectors.toMap.
